# Questions and answers for the FAQ section on this page. The build turns them
# into the visible section (at <!-- FAQ --> in the page) and FAQPage structured
# data. One "Q:" line and one "A:" line each; plain text, no double quotes.
Q: What does Beltway Demolition do?
A: Beltway Demolition is a demolition and site clearance contractor working across Maryland, Washington DC and Virginia. We handle demolition, environmental and abatement work, site clearance and grading, asset recovery and recycling, permits and project management, and hauling and disposal, so one contractor takes a site from standing structure to buildable ground.

Q: Where does Beltway Demolition work?
A: Across Maryland, Washington DC, Virginia and the surrounding Mid-Atlantic, including Baltimore, Annapolis, Washington DC, Montgomery and Prince George’s counties, Northern Virginia (Arlington, Alexandria, Fairfax and Loudoun), Frederick and Western Maryland, Southern Maryland and the Eastern Shore. If your site is outside that area, ask anyway. For the right job we travel well past the map.

Q: Is Beltway Demolition licensed and insured?
A: Beltway Demolition is backed by WMB LLC, which holds a construction contractor licence in the State of Maryland. Certificates of insurance are issued to the client and any additional insureds before mobilisation, and licence documentation is available with any quote.

Q: How do I get a demolition quote?
A: Send the site address, a few photos and what you need gone, by phone on +1 (703) 861-5354 or by email to info@beltwaydemo.com. We walk the site with you, then send a written quote with a fixed scope and a fixed price, including disposal, permits and any salvage credit.

Q: Do you handle demolition permits and utility disconnects?
A: Yes. Demolition permit applications, utility disconnect coordination and regulatory notifications are closed out before a machine is on site.

Q: What happens to the debris?
A: Debris, concrete and scrap are hauled to permitted and licensed facilities, and the disposal tickets and manifests are kept for your file. Structural steel and other salvage is recovered and sold, and its value comes off your invoice. Concrete is crushed and reused where practical.
